The method body directly determines the path. It always starts with the pattern assignment and the `re.search` guard; because the test string contains `${...}` and `&{...}`, the early return is skipped and execution continues to `new_s = deepcopy(s)`. The `for k, v in params.items()` loop runs in insertion order over `Stage`, `Cfg`, `Env`, and `Owner`, revisiting the loop head each time. For `Stage` and `Env`, `isinstance(v, dict)` is false, so only the two substitution lines run. For `Cfg` and `Owner`, the dict branch executes the multi-line md5/hex expression before those same two substitutions. After the loop, the code checks `isinstance(s, str_node)`; the test passes a plain `str`, so that branch is skipped and the function returns on the final line.

